ghpull:494 a bl:PullRequest, bl:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:label "deploy: make dagalog.no vocabulary IRIs dereferenceable via Caddy" ;
    bl:number 494 ;
    bl:state bl:Open ;
    bl:closesIssue ghissues:441 .

session:claudeSonnet5 a prov:SoftwareAgent ;
    rdfs:label "Claude Sonnet 5" .

session:pr494 a agp:AgentSession ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith session:claudeSonnet5 ;
    prov:used ghissues:441 ;
    prov:startedAtTime "2026-08-13T22:23:27+02:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-08-15T09:56:10Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

session:pr494Summary a agp:TranscriptSummary ;
    agp:summaryText "Issue #441 asked for dagalog.no vocabulary IRIs (bl:*/agp:* terms) to be dereferenceable, explicitly leaving the design open. The PR resolves this for the vocabulary case only: both backlog and agentprov namespaces are hash-URIs (terms are #fragments), so per the 'Cool URIs' convention the bare namespace IRI can be answered directly with the vocabulary Turtle file -- no 303 redirect needed, unlike resource IRIs (bl:Issue/bl:PullRequest instances), which are slash-URIs and need dagalog's own SPARQL endpoint plus a redirect, deferred to a new follow-up issue (#493) since it needs real engine involvement, not static hosting. Implementation is two Caddyfile handle blocks serving backlog/ontology/*.ttl, carved out of the forward_auth gate the same way /oauth2/* already is, since arbitrary external RDF clients will never sign in with Google. Content negotiation picks text/turtle by default and text/plain for browser Accept headers (deliberately not a rendered HTML page -- out of scope, noted as a non-goal). Validated against an isolated local Caddy container running the real ontology files, confirming correct Content-Type per Accept header and that the auth gate on other routes is untouched; the live deploy-caddy-1 container was only used for caddy adapt syntax checks via copied-in temp files, never reloaded." ;
    agp:reasoningFor ghpull:494 ;
    agp:decisionPoint session:pr494Decision ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy session:pr494 ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo session:claudeSonnet5 .

session:pr494Decision a agp:Decision ;
    agp:summaryText "Content negotiation between text/turtle and text/plain (for browser Accept headers) is implemented as two nested handle/handle blocks (matched-on-Accept, then unmatched default), each independently running its own header + file_server pair." ;
    agp:alternative "The first draft used two plain 'header Content-Type ...' directives in the same handle block (one Accept-matched, one unconditional default), written in source order default-then-override. Running caddy adapt on it revealed Caddy's Caddyfile adapter sorts same-name directives by matcher specificity rather than preserving source order: the unconditional line was emitted to run *after* the matched one, silently overwriting it for every request including browsers. Rejected once this was empirically confirmed in the adapted JSON output, in favor of nested handle blocks, which are mutually exclusive and order-preserving by construction -- the same guarantee the file's existing /oauth2/* vs. catch-all split already relies on." .
